## Exact default coding prompt/tool locations

The programmatic coding-agent SDK exports the profile factories at
`packages/coding-agent/src/core/sdk.ts:116-128`. Its initial default active tool names
are explicitly `['read', 'bash', 'edit', 'write']` at `sdk.ts:247-254`; settings and
caller allow/exclude lists can replace that set. Factory ordering for the default profile
is `read, bash, edit, write` at `packages/coding-agent/src/core/tools/index.ts:138-175`.
All seven built-in definitions and factories (`read`, `bash`, `edit`, `write`, `grep`,
`find`, `ls`) are enumerated at `index.ts:81-115,156-195`.

Prompt assembly is `packages/coding-agent/src/core/system-prompt.ts:8-25,27-162`:
`buildSystemPrompt` selects active tools, filters visible snippets, de-duplicates
guidelines in insertion order, adds the two unconditional guidelines, emits the ordered
prompt template, and appends the explicit working directory. Session-level rebuilding
and active-tool replacement are `packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts:897-943`
and `1023-1057`.

Each tool's prompt snippet/guidelines and definition/schema are in the following exact
locations:

* `core/tools/read.ts:21-32,209-221,356-357` — `readSchema`,
  `readToolSystemPromptContribution`, `createReadToolDefinition`, `createReadTool`.
* `core/tools/bash.ts:41-51,322-337,502-509` — bash schema/contribution and factories.
* `core/tools/edit.ts:34-66,298-310,441-442` — edit schema/contribution and factories.
* `core/tools/write.ts:15-25,187-199,272-273` — write schema/contribution and factories.
* `core/tools/grep.ts:24-44,128-138,388-389` — grep schema/contribution and factories.
* `core/tools/find.ts:29-42,123-133,378-379` — find schema/contribution and factories.
* `core/tools/ls.ts:14-24,100-110,228-229` — ls schema/contribution and factories.

The default snippets are, in order: `Read file contents`; `Execute bash commands
(ls, grep, find, etc.)`; `Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including
multiple disjoint edits in one call`; and `Create or overwrite files`. Their default
guidelines are carried by the corresponding `*ToolSystemPromptContribution` symbols;
`grep`, `find`, and `ls` contribute no guideline bullets. The default prompt always adds
`Be concise in your responses` and `Show file paths clearly when working with files`.
